Always generate Python code for this project that:

    Passes ALL pre-commit hooks and CI requirements:

        Includes: trailing-whitespace, end-of-file-fixer, check-yaml, check-json, check-toml, check-added-large-files, check-merge-conflict, check-case-conflict, check-docstring-first, debug-statements, requirements-txt-fixer, ruff, ruff-format, black, mypy, bandit.

    Strictly follows these standards:

        Python 3.13+ only.

        Code is part of a FastAPI backend project; use corresponding best practices for scalability, testability, maintainability, and clarity.

        Use type annotations everywhere (all functions, all variables as appropriate).

        Prefer modern Python idioms (e.g., match statement, Self type hint, | union operator instead of Union).

        Use only libraries present in tool.poetry.dependencies or tool.poetry.group.dev.dependencies in pyproject.toml.

        Format with black (line-length: 88, spaces for indent), always.

        Adhere to ruff and mypy: never write code that would fail lint or type checks, including all configuration options and ignores as set in pyproject.toml.

        Use double quotes for all strings; triple double quotes for all docstrings.

        Add Google-style docstrings to all public functions/classes.

        Never use wildcard imports, unused variables, ambiguous types, or other flagged patterns.

        No commented-out code, temporary debug, or uncommitted fixups; all code must be immediately committable.

        Use only explicit/safe error handling (no bare except).

        Follow security guidelines from bandit: do not introduce unsafe patterns, secrets, or insecure subprocess/crypto usage.

        Respect current architecture: Never restructure or reorganize the project, never add new folders/modules/files unless explicitly requested. Only create or edit documents/files if specifically asked by the user.

        Never create chat summaries or ancillary files (e.g., summary.md, overview.txt, etc.) unless directly instructed.

        Conform to [isort] import order per config (known-first-party, known-third-party).

        Default to readability and correctness over brevity or “cleverness”.

    Testing and documentation:

        Write unit tests with pytest for business logic code if requested.

        Never include or assume missing dependencies—only use those already defined.

    Industry standard best practices:

        Use best practices for Python, FastAPI, SQLAlchemy, and backend architecture.

        Prefer explicitness, modularity, context managers, pathlib over os.path, safe type narrowing, response/request validation, and Pydantic for schemas.

        Handle errors with specific exception types, no bare exceptions.

        Use logging instead of print.

        Ensure all public interfaces (API endpoints, services, utilities) are correctly documented and typed.
